title: 'pallet-xcm: migrate from LockableCurrency to fungible freezes'

doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |
    Migrates `pallet-xcm` off the deprecated `Currency`/`LockableCurrency` traits onto `fungible`.
    Assets locked locally for a remote unlocker are now held by a freeze under
    `FreezeReason::AssetLock` instead of the `py/xcmlk` lock. Part of #7226 / #226.

    Breaking `Config` changes:
    - `Currency: LockableCurrency` becomes `Currency: fungible::{Inspect, MutateFreeze}` with
      `Id = Self::RuntimeFreezeReason`.
    - `RuntimeFreezeReason` added, set it to the runtime's overarching freeze reason.
    - `OldCurrency: InspectLockableCurrency` added, only to release the legacy lock while
      migrating. Removable once every legacy lock is gone.

    Runtimes therefore need their fungible to expose that reason: `FreezeIdentifier =
    RuntimeFreezeReason` and a `MaxFreezes` covering its variant count. In-tree runtimes that had
    freezes disabled were updated; for chains whose `CurrencyMatcher` matches nothing this is a
    type-level change only, since they can never hold an XCM lock.

    Existing locks are converted by `migration::MigrateLocksToFreezes`, a multi-block migration
    registered on Rococo and Westend, and lazily on the next lock or unlock for chains without
    multi-block migration support. `LockedFungibles` is unchanged, so there is no storage version
    bump.

    Out of scope: a single fungible is still the only one supported. `fungibles` needs a new
    `LockedFungibles` layout (it stores no asset id) and a migration of its own, so it is left to a
    follow-up.

    Also: `prepare_lock` now rejects with `LockError::NoResources` when the account cannot take
    another freeze, fully unlocking no longer leaves a zero-amount lock behind, and `try_state`
    checks every `LockedFungibles` entry against the freeze, or against the legacy lock if the
    account is unmigrated.

